REFERENCE · NIXOS UPDATES
Keeping NixOS Current

The NixOS equivalent of `apt update && apt upgrade` or `pacman -Syu` — why
there isn't a single command, and the two-step model that replaces it.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
THE TWO-STEP MODEL
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

NixOS splits "what version could I have" from "what version do I have" into
two separate, deliberate steps. Arch's -Syu collapses both into one atomic
action; NixOS keeps them apart on purpose.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1 · Pull in newer package definitions                       ("-Sy" equivalent)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  cd /etc/nixos
  sudo nix flake update

Rewrites flake.lock to point at a newer nixpkgs commit. This makes newer
package versions available — nothing on the running system changes yet.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2 · Build and apply those packages                           ("-u" equivalent)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  sudo nixos-rebuild switch --flake /etc/nixos#nixos

Rebuilds the system using whatever's now pinned in flake.lock, and activates
it.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
ONE-LINER                                                   (closest to -Syu)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  cd /etc/nixos && sudo nix flake update && \
    sudo nixos-rebuild switch --flake .#nixos

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
WHY IT'S SPLIT
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

flake.lock is a record of an exact decision — "I want precisely this commit
of nixpkgs" — that persists across rebuilds until deliberately changed. That's
what makes a known-good pin possible to roll back to.

Running nixos-rebuild switch alone (no flake update first) rebuilds against
the same pinned versions already in flake.lock — nothing new gets pulled in.
This is the safe, everyday command for config-only changes (adding a package,
tweaking a Hyprland setting, etc.) — no surprises.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
RECOMMENDED CADENCE
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  - Config changes only → nixos-rebuild switch --flake /etc/nixos#nixos
      No flake update needed. Fast, deterministic.

  - Actual version bumps → every couple weeks to a month
      Deliberate, not constant. Low-risk on a stable 26.05 branch
      (backports/bugfixes only, not a release jump).

  - Before nixos-rebuild boot (not switch) after a flake update, especially
    anything graphics/Hyprland-related → always leave a way back without
    disturbing the current running session.

  - Commit flake.lock to git after every successful update+rebuild, so
    there's always a known-good pin to fall back to.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
CHECKING STATE                                          (no changes, just info)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  nixos-version

Reports the currently-running system's version/commit — confirms what's
actually running before deciding whether an update is worth doing.
